Liverpool entered the competition in the third round, where they were drawn against Second Division side [[Rotherham United F.C.|Rotherham United]] at [[Anfield|home]]. [[Igor Bišćan]] was sent off in the tie for a second bookable offence, moments after [[Emile Heskey]] had scored. [[Dietmar Hamann]] extended Liverpool's lead in the 73rd minute and a further goal by Heskey ensured their progress in the competition.<ref>{{cite news |title=Biscan blow sparks Liverpool into life |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/2995953/Biscan-blow-sparks-Liverpool-into-life.html |first=Huw |last=Turbervill |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=6 January 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref>

Liverpool's opponent in the fourth round was [[Leeds United F.C.|Leeds United]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Leeds meet old rivals on elite list |page=S1 |first=Nick |last=Szczepanik |newspaper=The Times |date=8 January 2001}}</ref> The match was played at [[Elland Road]] on 27 January 2001 in front of a near-capacity crowd of 37,108. The home team enjoyed much of the possession, but struggled to find a breakthrough as Liverpool's defence stood firm. Two minutes before full-time, Barmby, on as a substitute, scored the winning goal, rebounding a shot that came off the post. Barmby then turned provider for Liverpool's second, setting-up Heskey to score.<ref name="leed">{{cite news |title=Liverpool stun Leeds |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1138324.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=27 January 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref>

Anfield hosted [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] in the fifth round. It was the start of a decisive week for Liverpool, as they faced [[A.S. Roma|Roma]] midweek in the [[2000–01 UEFA Cup|UEFA Cup]], then [[Birmingham City F.C.|Birmingham City]] in the [[2001 Football League Cup final]].<ref name="city">{{cite news |title=Liverpool too slick for City |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1177440.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=18 February 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref> Liverpool were awarded a penalty after five minutes, as goalkeeper [[Nicky Weaver]] fouled [[Vladimír Šmicer]] inside the 18-yard box. [[Jari Litmanen]] converted the spot kick to give Liverpool the lead, which quickly became 2–0 when Heskey's shot found its way past Weaver. [[Andrei Kanchelskis]]' goal in the 28th minute halved the scoreline, but Šmicer and [[Markus Babbel]] each scored in the second half to put Liverpool in a commanding lead. City persisted and in stoppage time scored their second goal of the match; [[Shaun Goater]]'s deflected shot did enough to beat goalkeeper [[Sander Westerveld]].<ref name="city"/>

Liverpool travelled to [[Prenton Park]] to play [[Tranmere Rovers F.C.|Tranmere Rovers]] in the sixth round. [[Danny Murphy (footballer, born 1977)|Danny Murphy]] and [[Michael Owen]] each scored in a first half in which the visitors dominated play.<ref name="tran">{{cite news |title=Liverpool show appetite for fight |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/liverpool/4776618/Liverpool-show-appetite-for-fight.html |first=Henry |last=Winter |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=12 March 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref> [[Steve Yates (footballer, born 1970)|Steve Yates]] pulled a goal back for Tranmere after half-time, but in the 52nd minute [[Steven Gerrard]] headed-in a cross to restore Liverpool's two-goal advantage. A mistake by [[Robbie Fowler]] gifted substitute [[Wayne Allison]] the chance to score, but the striker made amends as he converted a penalty kick in the 81st minute.<ref name="tran"/> In the semi-final, Liverpool faced [[Wycombe Wanderers F.C.|Wycombe Wanderers]] at [[Villa Park]]. Goals from Heskey and Fowler and a consolation scored by Wycombe's captain [[Keith Ryan]] ensured Liverpool won 2–1 and earnt a place in the final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool end Wycombe's fight |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1264608.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=8 April 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref>

==Pre-match==

===Stadium changes===
[[File:Stadiwm y MileniwmLB02.JPG|thumb|225px|upright|In addition to hosting the FA Cup Final, the [[Millennium Stadium]] acted as the venue for the [[FA Charity Shield|Charity Shield]], and all major [[Football League]] trophy and play-off finals.<ref>{{cite news |title=	FA opts for Cardiff final |url=http://www.scotsman.com/sport/football/fa-opts-for-cardiff-final-1-641588 |newspaper=The Scotsman |location=Edinburgh |date=5 January 2001 |access-date=26 February 2016}}</ref>]]
In October 2000, [[Wembley Stadium (1923)|Wembley Stadium]], the final's traditional venue, was closed in anticipation of major redevelopment.<ref>{{cite news |title=Timeline: The new Wembley |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1326723.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=21 February 2006 |access-date=26 February 2016}}</ref> Having considered alternative venues such as [[Twickenham Stadium|Twickenham]], [[Murrayfield]] and [[Villa Park]], the FA announced in January 2001 that the next three Cup finals would be staged at Cardiff's [[Millennium Stadium]].<ref name="finalda">{{cite news |title=Millennium showpieces |page=71 |first=Mark |last=Bradley |newspaper=Coventry Telegraph |date=4 January 2001}}</ref> This therefore marked the first time the showpiece event was held outside England.<ref name="finalda"/> In the lead-up to the final, FA chief executive [[Adam Crozier]] acknowledged concerns over transport and the quality of the pitch, saying, "We've done a lot of work with Cardiff and hopefully it will be a great occasion, but we'll take a view about how it's worked after the final and decide whether to still hold the match there."<ref>{{cite news |title=Crozier: Cardiff on trial for Cup final |page=1 |first=Nick |last=Townsend |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=6 May 2001}}</ref> A new pitch was re-laid in May, and the police made attempts to ease traffic on the [[M4 motorway|M4]] by opening three more turn-offs than they did for the League Cup final.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cardiff prepares for moment of cup final glory |page=4 |first=Roger |last=Dobson |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=10 May 2001}}</ref>

The finalists received a total allocation of approximately 52,000 tickets, which was an estimated increase of 10% on previous Wembley finals.<ref name="timtick">{{cite news |title=FA kicks out Cup Final freeloaders |page=40 |first=Matt |last=Dickinson |newspaper=The Times |date=26 January 2001}}</ref> Seat prices for the final exceeded [[Pound sterling|£]]70,<ref name="timtick"/> though some ticket touts charged as much as £1,000.<ref>{{cite news |title=Police seize fake tickets |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/COUNTDOWN+TO+THE+FA+CUP+FINAL%3A+POLICE+SEIZE+FAKE+TICKETS.-a074374787 |first=David |last=Mather |newspaper=The Mirror |location=London |date=10 May 2001 |access-date=26 February 2016}}</ref> The cheapest tickets cost £20; the rest were priced at £40 and £55.<ref name="timtick"/>

===Build-up===
Arsenal were appearing in the final of the FA Cup for the 14th time, their first in over three years. They had won the cup seven times previously (in [[1930 FA Cup final|1930]], [[1936 FA Cup final|1936]], [[1950 FA Cup final|1950]], [[1971 FA Cup final|1971]], [[1979 FA Cup final|1979]], [[1993 FA Cup final|1993]] and [[1998 FA Cup final|1998]]) and were beaten in the final six times.<ref>{{cite web |title=Arsenal's complete cup finals |url=http://www.statto.com/football/teams/arsenal/history/cup-finals |publisher=Statto Organisation |access-date=27 February 2016 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304082911/http://www.statto.com/football/teams/arsenal/history/cup-finals |archive-date=4 March 2016 |df=dmy-all }}</ref> By comparison, Liverpool were making their 12th appearance in a FA Cup final. The club won the cup five times (in [[1965 FA Cup final|1965]], [[1974 FA Cup final|1974]], [[1986 FA Cup final|1986]], [[1989 FA Cup final|1989]], [[1992 FA Cup final|1992]]) and lost six finals, most recent of which against [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] in [[1996 FA Cup final|1996]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Liverpool's complete cup finals |url=http://www.statto.com/football/teams/liverpool/history/cup-finals |publisher=Statto Organisation |access-date=27 February 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304100718/http://www.statto.com/football/teams/liverpool/history/cup-finals |archive-date=4 March 2016 |url-status=live |df=dmy-all }}</ref> Arsenal and Liverpool had previously met thirteen times in the FA Cup, including four replays.<ref name="statf">{{cite web |title=Arsenal's head-to-head comparison with Liverpool |url=http://www.statto.com/football/teams/arsenal/liverpool/head-to-head |publisher=Statto Organisation |access-date=26 February 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304075427/http://www.statto.com/football/teams/arsenal/liverpool/head-to-head |archive-date=4 March 2016 |url-status=live |df=dmy-all }}</ref> Arsenal had a slender advantage in those meetings, winning five times to Liverpool's four.<ref name="statf"/> Both clubs were involved in the longest ever semi-final in FA Cup history in 1980, which required three replays after the original tie ended goalless.<ref name="gual">{{cite news |title=Joy of Six: Liverpool v Arsenal matches |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2012/mar/02/joy-of-six-liverpool-arsenal |first=Scott |last=Murray |newspaper=The Guardian |date=2 March 2012 |access-date=27 February 2016}}</ref> [[Brian Talbot]]'s goal at [[Highfield Road]] earnt Arsenal a 1–0 victory.<ref name="gual"/>

[[File:Houllier, Gérard.jpg|thumb|220px|upright|left|Liverpool manager [[Gérard Houllier]] was on course to win multiple trophies in the 2000–01 season.]]
The last meeting between the two teams had been in the Premier League on 23 December 2000. Liverpool recorded a 4–0 win at Anfield, their third-straight victory in all competitions.<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool keep dream alive |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/liverpool/2995199/Liverpool-keep-dream-alive.html |first=Colin |last=Malam |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=24 December 2000 |access-date=26 February 2016}}</ref> [[Gérard Houllier]], the manager of Liverpool, enjoyed success in the cup competitions during the 2000–01 season; under his management, the club ended their six-year spell without silverware by winning the League Cup,<ref>{{cite news |title=Blues shot down as Liverpool lift cup |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/worthington_cup/1187654.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=25 February 2001 |access-date=26 February 2016}}</ref> and in the calendar year defeated Roma, [[FC Porto|Porto]] and [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] to reach the [[2001 UEFA Cup final]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Liverpool's Uefa Cup run |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/uefa_cup/1331304.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=15 May 2001 |access-date=26 February 2016}}</ref> Houllier was indifferent that his side were considered the underdogs in the FA Cup final, and told reporters, "We have great work ethic and team ethic. There is a great desire to achieve something as a club."<ref name="houpre">{{cite news |first=Sean |last=Ingle |title=Heskey to start tomorrow |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/11/newsstory.sport3 |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=11 May 2001 |access-date=26 February 2016}}</ref> He confirmed in his pre-match press conference that Heskey would start the final, though had yet to make a final decision over who would partner the England forward.<ref name="houpre"/>

Arsenal manager [[Arsène Wenger]] admitted his team had underachieved during the season, but took criticism of failing to challenge Manchester United in the league and progressing further in the [[UEFA Champions League]] as a "compliment to the club".<ref name="wengpre">{{cite news |title=Wenger driven by the fear of failure |page=31 |first=Tim |last=Rich |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=12 May 2001}}</ref> He spoke in favour of moving FA Cup ties midweek to prevent fixture congestion, because the current system was too demanding for the big clubs: "In England a team that goes for the FA Cup automatically has a problem in the championship, especially if they play in the Champions League. There are then so many games something has to be sacrificed and I have sacrificed points in the championship to the FA Cup."<ref name="wengpre"/> When asked whether reducing the number of teams in the UEFA Champions League would help, Wenger commented it "...&nbsp;would mean less money and no one with the wages we pay can accept a drop in income. As for cutting the Premiership – they have done that in France and now those teams not involved in the cups are complaining."<ref name="wengpre"/>

The match attracted considerable media interest because of the number of foreigners involved.<ref name="fore">{{cite news |title=Abide with moi, boyo! |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/%5B1%5D+FOOTBALL%3A+FA+CUP+FINAL+2001%3A+ARSENAL+v+LIVERPOOL+-+ABIDE+WITH...-a074461248 |first1=John |last1=Dillon |first2=David  |last2=Maddock |newspaper=The Mirror |location=London |date=12 May 2001 |access-date=27 February 2016}}</ref> It marked the first time that the two managers of opposing sides were born outside the British Isles, and it was anticipated the final would receive a large overseas audience because of the inclusion of several international players.<ref name="fore"/> Broadcast in 70 countries, viewing figures for the match totalled close to 600&nbsp;million.<ref>{{cite web |title=Economic Impact of the FA Cup and Other Football Matches |url=http://formerly.cardiff.gov.uk/ObjView.asp?Object_ID=10778 |publisher=Cardiff Council |format=PDF |year=2001 |access-date=30 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160416224446/http://formerly.cardiff.gov.uk/ObjView.asp?Object_ID=10778 |archive-date=16 April 2016 |url-status=dead |df=dmy-all }}</ref>

==Match==
===Summary===
Both clubs lined up in a traditional [[4–4–2 formation]]: a four-man [[Defender (association football)|defence]] (comprising two [[centre-back]]s and [[full-back (association football)|left and right full-backs]]), four [[midfielder]]s (two in the [[central midfielder|centre]], and one on each [[wide midfielder|wing]]) and two [[centre forward]]s.<ref>{{cite news |title=Houllier's way the right path to glory |page=S3 |first=Glenn |last=Moore |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=14 May 2001}}</ref> Wenger opted to pair [[Gilles Grimandi]] with Vieira in midfield and Wiltord up front with Henry, leaving Bergkamp on the substitutes' bench.<ref>{{cite news |title=Keown and Henry left frustrated by weakest links in team |page=S7 |first=Oliver |last=Kay |newspaper=The Times |date=14 May 2001}}</ref> [[Ashley Cole]] played in defence, ahead of [[Sylvinho]], who did not feature in the matchday squad.<ref name="bbcclock">{{cite news |title=FA Cup final clockwatch |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1326723.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=12 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref> For Liverpool, Houllier named Owen in the starting line-up, and chose Šmicer and Murphy to play in midfield.<ref name="rudd">{{cite news |title=Cool Owen shows his ability to shine on the big occasion |page=S7 |first=Alyson |last=Rudd |newspaper=The Times |date=14 May 2001}}</ref> [[Gary McAllister]], [[Patrik Berger]] and Fowler began the final as substitutes.<ref name="rudd"/>

Arsenal in their usual home strip of red shirts and white shorts kicked off the match and immediately won a corner, which was dealt with by Westerveld.<ref name="bbcclock"/> A run by Heskey six minutes later resulted in the player taking a tumble under [[Gilles Grimandi]]'s challenge, but his appeals for a penalty were ignored by referee Dunn.<ref name="bbcclock"/> Arsenal began to dominate play, with Vieira at the heart of their best moves. The midfielder won a challenge with Heskey in the 17th minute and sent the ball in the direction of [[Freddie Ljungberg]], who in turn passed it to Henry. The Frenchman went around Westerveld and shot the ball goalwards, which was cleared off the line by [[Stéphane Henchoz]].<ref name="bbcrep">{{cite news |title=Owen shatters Arsenal in Cup final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/1324699.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=12 May 2001 |access-date=31 December 2009}}</ref> Television replays later showed the ball hitting Henchoz's arm before going wide; although Henry appealed for a penalty, it was turned down as the incident was missed by both the referee and his assistant.<ref name="bbcrep"/> Owen came close to scoring in the 20th minute, but for his shot to be blocked by [[Martin Keown]].<ref name="bbcrep"/> Arsenal continued to find the best openings, but struggled to split open the Liverpool defence. A long range effort by Grimandi was easily saved by Westerveld as the final approached the half-hour mark, and a duel between Wiltord and [[Jamie Carragher]] on the right side resulted in a Liverpool corner.<ref name="bbcclock"/> Two minutes before the interval Henry was penalised for drifting into an offside position, having collected a long pass.<ref name="guarcl">{{cite news |title=Arsenal 1–2 Liverpool |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/12/minutebyminute.sport |first=Matt |last=Hughes |newspaper=The Guardian |date=12 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref>

Liverpool resumed play and won a free-kick in the 48th minute; Murphy's delivery found Heskey, whose header forced a save from [[David Seaman]]. Arsenal enjoyed their best spell of the match soon afterwards, but failed to make use of their set-pieces.<ref name="bbcclock"/> A free-kick taken by Pires was easily handled by Westerveld, and nothing came out of the resulting corner.<ref name="bbcclock"/> Pires and Henry combined in attack for Arsenal and the latter came close to scoring, had the Liverpool goalkeeper not intervened. The ball rebounded to Cole who shot goalwards, but [[Sami Hyypiä]] cleared off the line.<ref name="bbcclock"/> Hamann was shown a yellow card for fouling Vieira in the 57th minute, and Houllier responded by replacing him with McAllister four minutes later.<ref name="bbcclock"/> The change had the desired effect as it brought composure to Liverpool's play, particularly in midfield. In the 62nd minute Ljungberg received a yellow card for a challenge on Šmicer. Arsenal squandered another chance, this time two minutes before the 70; Henry outpaced Henchoz and his rebounded shot found Ljungberg in the penalty area. The midfielder's effort, a chip over the advancing Westerveld, was cleared off the line by Hyypiä.<ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup Final: Owen's double take stuns unlucky Arsenal |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/liverpool/3005224/FA-Cup-Final-Owens-double-take-stuns-unlucky-Arsenal.html |first=Colin |last=Malam |newspaper=The Sunday Telegraph |location=London |date=13 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref>

With 19 minutes left, Arsenal finally scored. A poor clearance by Westerveld fell to Grimandi, who passed the ball to Pires. Ljungberg received it and rounded the goalkeeper to score, much to Wenger's delight.<ref name="guarcl"/> Henry missed a chance to give Arsenal a two-goal lead in the 74th minute, as his shot was point-blank saved by Westerveld and on the follow-up cleared by Hyypiä.<ref name="bbcclock"/> Both managers made changes in the final period of the game; [[Ray Parlour]] came on for Wiltord in order to protect Arsenal's lead, whereas Liverpool made an attacking double substitution – Fowler and Berger on for Šmicer and Murphy.<ref name="bbcclock"/> Liverpool survived the onslaught and found a foothold in the game when Owen equalised with eight minutes left. Arsenal failed to clear substitute McAllister's free-kick, and Owen pounced with a right-foot finish past Seaman from eight yards.<ref name="bbcrep"/> Liverpool's comeback was completed six minutes later in the 88th minute, with extra time looming; Owen was released down the left by a weighted long ball pass from Berger, with the type of vision which had been earlier missing from their play, and he outpaced both [[Tony Adams]] and Dixon before shooting low and accurately past Seaman, beating him at the far post.<ref name="guarcl"/> Liverpool held on to their lead for the few minutes remaining to win the final.<ref name="guarcl"/>

