==Post-match and aftermath==
Houllier was disappointed with Liverpool's start and noticed his players struggled with the humidity.<ref>{{cite news |title=Houllier celebrates Cardiff triumph |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/1327336.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=12 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref> He nevertheless saw set-pieces as an opportunity to score goals from and felt the equaliser turned the final in his team's favour: "Suddenly the confidence switches to the other camp. They're affected, they become a bit unsettled, and you keep going."<ref name="richw"/> Houllier revealed he gave a frank assessment of the task facing his players before the match; although in his words Arsenal were "...&nbsp;probably a bit better than us, probably more mature, more experienced, more ability in some areas", dealing with setbacks would make the difference on the day.<ref name="richw"/> He dismissed accusations that Liverpool were boring, rather describing his team as difficult to beat.<ref>{{cite news |title=Tables turn as Liverpool produce late knockout |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/liverpool/3005314/Tables-turn-as-Liverpool-produce-late-knockout.html |first=Henry |last=Winter |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=15 May 2001 |access-date=17 June 2015}}</ref> Owen, the match winner, felt he answered his critics by scoring left-footed: "It's nice to prove people wrong when they say you haven't got a left foot. I was supposed to be the worst header and the worst player with my left foot in the league, and how could an England player play when they haven't got a left foot.&nbsp;... It does go to show that I have improved and I am improving."<ref>{{cite news |title=I've nothing more to prove, says Owen |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/l/liverpool/1328141.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=13 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref>
[[File:Thierry Henry (498018484).jpg|thumb|200px|upright|[[Thierry Henry]]'s performance received mixed reviews by the British press.]]
Wenger lamented Arsenal's inability to make possession count, saying: "It has happened all season. We don't finish."<ref name="lawt">{{cite news |title=Clinical Owen takes Arsenal to finishing school |page=S3 |first=James |last=Lawton |newspaper=The Independent |location=London |date=14 May 2001}}</ref> He criticised the referee for not sending off Henchoz, especially as linesman Kevin Pike told him the Liverpool defender had committed a foul.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger: Ref Dunn us right in |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/Football%3A+Wenger%3A+Ref+Dunn+us+right+in.-a074468231 |newspaper=Sunday Mail |location=Glasgow |date=14 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref> On reflection of the season, Wenger disagreed it was one of failure – "It is not easy to get to the quarter-finals of the Champions League, the final of the FA Cup and to finish in the top three of the Premiership in the same season", but admitted he needed to make signings to strengthen the squad.<ref>{{cite news |title=Wenger's trust again betrayed by Arsenal's lack of killer instinct |page=S7 |first=Matt |last=Dickinson |newspaper=The Times |date=14 May 2001}}</ref> Henry called for changes to Arsenal's forward line and felt the team would benefit from a "fox in the box", a player who would stay in and around the penalty area to score.<ref name="foxbox">{{cite news |title=Henry: We need box fox |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/teams/a/arsenal/1329408.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=14 May 2001 |access-date=27 February 2016}}</ref> "When I make wide runs and put in crosses there is often no-one there to put the ball in the net. Owen was the hero because he is always in the right place. We need a goalscorer like that", he concluded.<ref name="foxbox"/>

{{Quote box|width=250px|bgcolor=#FFFFF0 |align=left|quote="On some occasions the will to win is more important than the skill to win."|salign = left|source=[[Gérard Houllier]] speaking after the game<ref name="richw">{{cite news |title=Owen fast forward to next blockbuster |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/14/sport.comment1 |first=Richard |last=Williams |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=14 May 2001 |access-date=17 June 2015}}</ref>}}

Journalists and pundits reviewing the final praised Liverpool's tenacity; radio commentator [[Alan Green (broadcaster)|Alan Green]] wrote in his ''[[The News Letter|News Letter]]'' column of 14 May 2001: "Simply, they never give up and when you have a player like Michael Owen within your ranks you believe that any situation can be rescued, as it was in the magnificent Millennium Stadium."<ref>{{cite news |title='Old Empire' striking back |page=60 |first=Alan |last=Green |newspaper=The News Letter |date=14 May 2001}}</ref> ''[[The Guardian]]'' correspondent [[David Lacey]] declared "The Owen of France '98 was reborn in Cardiff,"<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/14/match.sport |title=Five minutes that turned the world upside down |first=David |last=Lacey |newspaper=The Guardian |date=14 May 2001 |access-date=6 April 2015}}</ref> while [[Hugh McIlvanney]] of ''[[The Sunday Times]]'' called Owen "the master executioner of English football." McIlvanney felt over the 90 minutes the Arsenal team were "unlucky to lose", lauding Vieira's show in midfield.<ref>{{cite news |title=Gunned down by smiling assassin |page=S3 |first=Hugh |last=McIlvanney |newspaper=The Sunday Times |date=13 May 2001}}</ref> James Lawton's match report in ''[[The Independent]]'' was not as empathetic; although in praise of Henry's performance his indecision compared to Owen, demonstrated how "effect, not style, is everything" in football.<ref name="lawt"/> Clive White of ''[[The Herald (Glasgow)|The Herald]]'' criticised Henry's lack of end product, using the final and Arsenal's European failure against [[Valencia CF|Valencia]] to demonstrate how he would never be considered a "natural goalscorer".<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-23848090.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150924191246/http://www.highbeam.com/doc/1P2-23848090.html |url-status=usurped |archive-date=24 September 2015 |title=Owen shows how it's done; No hoorays for Henry as he fails to breach defence |first=Clive |last=White |newspaper=The Herald |location=Glasgow |date=14 May 2001 |access-date=6 April 2015|via=[[HighBeam Research]] |url-access=subscription }}</ref> [[Ron Atkinson]], writing in his tactics column for ''The Guardian'' noted Liverpool's winning goal only came about because of Arsenal's eagerness to attack, which left gaps in defence.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/14/facup.sport2 |title=How a moment's lapse lost the Cup for Arsenal |first=Ron |last=Atkinson |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=14 May 2001 |access-date=6 April 2015}}</ref>

The final was broadcast live in the United Kingdom by both [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] and [[Sky Sports]], with the former providing free-to-air coverage and Sky Sports 2 being the pay-TV alternative.<ref>{{cite news |title=The sporting week |url=http://www.thefreelibrary.com/The+sporting+week%3A+Richard+Morgan+picks+the+highlights+on+TV.-a074461565 |first=Richard |last=Morgan |newspaper=The Mirror |location=London |date=12 May 2001 |access-date=30 December 2014}}</ref> ITV held the majority of the viewership, with an overnight peak audience of 7.8&nbsp;million viewers.<ref>{{cite news|title=Ratings – BBC1 hits right note with Euro singsong |url=http://www.broadcastnow.co.uk/ratings-bbc-1-hits-right-note-with-euro-singsong/1175283.article |date=18 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015 |journal=Broadcast |publisher=Top Right Group|url-access=subscription }}</ref> Four days after the final Liverpool beat [[Deportivo Alavés|Alavés]] by five goals to four, to win the [[UEFA Cup]] and complete a treble of cup victories.<ref>{{cite news |title=Houllier hails brilliant Reds |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/uefa_cup/1335207.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=17 May 2001 |access-date=27 February 2016}}</ref> Victory against [[Charlton Athletic F.C.|Charlton Athletic]] on 20 May 2001 ensured Liverpool finished third in the Premier League and with that earn a place in the [[2001–02 UEFA Champions League]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/21/match.sport |title=Houllier's rallying call produces final flourish from the men of May |first=David |last=Lacey |newspaper=The Guardian |date=21 May 2001 |access-date=18 June 2015}}</ref> Arsenal ended the season as league runners-up; a draw against [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]] was enough for them to secure second spot.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2001/may/16/match.sport |title=Silver medal for Gunners |first=John |last=Wardle |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |date=16 May 2001 |access-date=17 June 2015}}</ref>

In his autobiography released a few years later, Gerrard reflected that Liverpool were fortunate to win, but was critical of Arsenal's "bitter" reaction: "Wenger and Ljungberg kept complaining about the hand-balls. Get real, boys. That's football. Grow up."<ref>{{cite book |first=Steven |last=Gerrard |title=Gerrard: My Autobiography |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=ANmFyoITK_EC&pg=PA204 |page=204 |publisher=Random House |year=2012 |isbn=978-1-4481-5446-3}}</ref> He reserved special praise for his opponent Vieira however, saying a few days after the game: "It was my job to try and stop him, but he definitely got the better of me that day. He's such a great player. He's so fit, and he dictates the pace of a game. There are a lot of things you can learn just by watching him."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/liverpool/3006365/Gerrard-salutes-Vieira-the-master.html |title=Gerrard salutes Vieira the master |first=Matt |last=Lawton |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=30 May 2001 |access-date=20 November 2015}}</ref> Assessing his career in a column for the ''Telegraph'', Owen wrote that winning the cup was "...the best day of my career, the game I look back on more than any other and think it was the most exhilarating experience I ever had playing football."<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/10836532/Michael-Owen-Winning-the-FA-Cup-with-Liverpool-remains-the-highlight-of-my-career.html |title=Winning the FA Cup with Liverpool remains the highlight of my career |first=Michael |last=Owen |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |location=London |date=14 May 2014 |access-date=27 February 2016}}</ref>

